Date: Fri, 24 Oct 2026 05:10:00 -0300
Subject: Wayland: a window that holds its views side by side is shown at one view's width

A program that draws both eyes of a stereo surface itself puts them side
by side into one window twice as wide and declares the surface as full
side by side. The compositor (Stereo KWin, kde_stereo_content_v1 from
version 2) takes a declared surface's wp_viewport destination, when one
is set, as the size of one view, and Qt gives every window a viewport
the size of the window; the pair was therefore shown twice as wide,
each eye stretched over the whole window.

A window may now say how many views it holds side by side, with the
dynamic property _q_stereo_side_by_side_views (an int, the count); its
viewport destination is then its width divided by that count. Windows
without the property are unchanged. The property is read when the
viewport is updated, so a program sets it before it shows the window,
or resizes the window after setting it.

Assisted-by: Claude Fable 5.1
---
--- a/src/plugins/platforms/wayland/qwaylandwindow.cpp
+++ b/src/plugins/platforms/wayland/qwaylandwindow.cpp
@@ -565,8 +565,15 @@
 
 void QWaylandWindow::updateViewport()
 {
-    if (!surfaceSize().isEmpty())
-        mViewport->setDestination(surfaceSize());
+    QSize destination = surfaceSize();
+    // A window that holds its views side by side (_q_stereo_side_by_side_views, their count), as a program
+    // that draws both eyes of a declared stereo surface into one window twice as wide, is shown at one
+    // view's width: the compositor takes a declared surface's viewport as the size of one view.
+    const int views = window()->property("_q_stereo_side_by_side_views").toInt();
+    if (views > 1)
+        destination.setWidth(destination.width() / views);
+    if (!destination.isEmpty())
+        mViewport->setDestination(destination);
 }
 
 void QWaylandWindow::setGeometryFromApplyConfigure(const QPoint &globalPosition, const QSize &sizeWithMargins)
